 # Topological Sort for Cell Projection
 
-Also called: Back-to-front sort using directed graph, Directed graph sort, Exact Meshed Polyhedra Visibility Ordering, Exact Visibility Ordering for Cell Projection, MPVO (Maximal Polyhedra Visibility Ordering), MPVO algorithm, Visibility Sorting (Topological Sort for Convex Grids)
+Also called: MPVO, MPVO algorithm, Meshed Polyhedra Visibility Ordering, Maximal Polyhedra Visibility Ordering, Exact Meshed Polyhedra Visibility Ordering, Exact Visibility Ordering for Cell Projection
 
 ## Overview
-Topological Sort for Cell Projection (MPVO) is an $O(n)$ algorithm for computing a back-to-front visibility ordering of convex polyhedral cells in a convex grid [S1][S2, book][S3, unofficial]. It solves the occlusion ordering problem required for correct alpha-blending in object-order volume rendering by constructing a directed graph of pairwise occlusion relations between adjacent cells and performing a topological sort [S1][S3, unofficial]. Unlike [Tetrahedral Volume Rendering](820-tetrahedral-volume-rendering.md) or [Projected Tetrahedra](877-projected-tetrahedra.md) which may rely on simpler centroid or distance-based heuristics, MPVO guarantees a correct visibility order for convex meshes by respecting the transitive occlusion chain along viewing rays [S1][S3, unofficial]. It fails on non-convex meshes because it ignores occlusion across empty space between non-adjacent cells [S1][S3, unofficial].
+Topological Sort for Cell Projection (MPVO) is an $O(n)$ algorithm for computing a back-to-front visibility ordering of convex polyhedral cells in a convex grid [S1][S2, unofficial][S3, book]. It solves the occlusion ordering problem required for correct alpha-blending in object-order volume rendering by constructing a directed graph of pairwise occlusion relations between adjacent cells and performing a topological sort [S1][S3, book]. Unlike [Tetrahedral Volume Rendering](820-tetrahedral-volume-rendering.md) or [Projected Tetrahedra](877-projected-tetrahedra.md) which may rely on simpler centroid or distance-based heuristics, MPVO guarantees a correct visibility order for convex meshes by respecting the transitive occlusion chain along viewing rays [S1][S3, book]. It fails on non-convex meshes because it ignores occlusion across empty space between non-adjacent cells [S1][S3, book].
 
 ## How it works
-**Inputs:** A convex grid of convex polyhedral cells; a viewpoint $V$ (for perspective) or a projection direction vector $\vec{d}$ (for orthogonal) [S1][S3, unofficial].
-**Outputs:** A list of cells sorted in back-to-front order [S1][S3, unofficial].
+**Inputs:** A convex grid of convex polyhedral cells; a viewpoint $V$ (for perspective) or a projection direction vector $\vec{d}$ (for orthogonal) [S1][S3, book].
+**Outputs:** A list of cells sorted in back-to-front order, where an occluded cell precedes an occluder [S1][S3, book].
 
 **Data Structures:**
-1.  **Cell Node:** Contains `incount` (integer, number of incoming edges), `visited` (boolean), `cycle_detection` (boolean), `outgoing` (list of cell references it occludes), and `incoming` (list of cell references that occlude it) [S1][S3, unofficial].
-2.  **Directed Graph:** Implicitly defined by adjacency. Nodes are cells. An edge exists from cell $B$ to cell $A$ if $B <_{vp} A$ [S1].
-3.  **Queue / Sink List:** A FIFO queue for cells with `incount == 0` (Kahn's variant) or a list of sink cells with no outgoing edges (DFS variant) [S1][S3, unofficial].
