 **Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-*   **Visibility Cycles:** If the graph contains a cycle, the topological sort fails. In DFS, this is detected if a neighbor is currently in the recursion stack (`cycle_detection` is true) [S3, unofficial]. In the Kahn's algorithm variant (queue-based), this is detected if the queue empties before all cells are output [S1]. Cycles imply no global visibility order exists; cells must be subdivided or per-pixel sorting used [S1][S3, unofficial].
-*   **Non-Convex Meshes:** The algorithm assumes convexity. If the mesh is non-convex, viewing rays may exit and re-enter the volume, creating occlusion relations not captured by shared faces [S1][S3, unofficial]. MPVO fails here [S3, unofficial].
-*   **Non-Planar Faces:** If a face is non-planar (e.g., in curvilinear grids), a ray may leave and re-enter the same cell across the same face, making visibility sorting impossible for that cell [S1].
+*   **Visibility Cycles:** If the graph contains a cycle, the topological sort fails [S3, book]. In DFS, this is detected if a neighbor is currently in the recursion stack (`cycle_detection` is true) [S3, book]. In the Kahn's algorithm variant (queue-based), this is detected if the queue empties before all cells are output [S1]. Cycles imply no global visibility order exists; cells must be subdivided or per-pixel sorting used [S1][S3, book].
+*   **Non-Convex Grids:** If the grid is non-convex, viewing rays may leave the volume through an external face, cross empty space, and re-enter another cell [S1]. MPVO only considers shared faces, so it misses these cross-gap occlusions [S1][S3, book].
+*   **Complexity Accounting:** Each cell is treated twice during initialization and once in the loop; each edge is treated once in initialization and once in the loop [S1].
 
 ## Subtopics
 
-### Approximate MPVONC Sort
-An approximate, faster sorting algorithm for real-time rendering on non-convex or large datasets [S2, book][S3, unofficial]. It starts with an $O(n \log n)$ sort of cells with exterior faces, keyed by the distance of their centroids to the viewpoint [S2, book][S3, unofficial]. A depth-first search is then conducted from these sorted exterior cells [S3, unofficial]. It is used when correct sorts (like MPVO) are too slow for real-time interaction with large datasets [S2, book].
+### [A-buffer visibility sort for non-convex grids](1057-a-buffer-visibility-sort-for-non-convex-grids.md)
+An algorithm to sort exterior faces for volume rendering on general non-convex grids using an A-buffer to manage pixel-level intersection lists, avoiding true visibility sort complexities while ensuring correct hardware rendering. It sorts exterior faces by centroid depth, scan-converts them, and uses the A-buffer to extract extra occlusion relations across ray gaps (empty space between cells). These extra relations are processed during the topological sort. Time complexity is $O(n + b^2)$ for pairwise boundary tests, or $O(n + bp)$ when combined with balanced BSP trees.
 
-### A-buffer visibility sort for non-convex grids
-An algorithm for general non-convex grids that avoids true global visibility sort complexities by using an A-buffer [S1]. It sorts exterior faces by centroid depth, scan-converts them, and uses the A-buffer to extract extra occlusion relations across ray gaps (empty space between cells) [S1]. These extra relations are processed during the topological sort [S1]. It does not produce a true global visibility sort but ensures image correctness by matching the A-buffer rays to pixel sample rays [S1]. Time complexity is $O(b \log b + wh + a + n)$, where $b$ is exterior faces, $w,h$ image dimensions, $a$ projected area of external faces, and $n$ relations across shared faces [S1].
+### [Power Distance Sort](1060-power-distance-sort.md)
+A sorting method based on power distance described by Cignoni et al. and Wittenbrim. It is fast with small constant computations but not guaranteed to produce accurate visibility ordering unless the mesh is a Delaunay triangulation. Simple schemes like this may fail from certain viewpoints or viewing directions.
 
-### Power Distance Sort
-A sorting method based on power distance [S3, unofficial]. It is fast with small constant computations but not guaranteed to produce accurate visibility ordering unless the mesh is a Delaunay triangulation [S3, unofficial]. Simple schemes like this may fail from certain viewpoints or viewing directions [S3, unofficial].
+### [Per-Pixel Sorting (General Variant)](1061-per-pixel-sorting-general-variant.md)
+Variants that sort all cells projecting onto a pixel at render time, just prior to compositing. They handle visibility cycles gracefully since a visibility sort always exists for each pixel center even if no global sort exists. They usually spend greater effort on per-pixel sorting since exterior faces are a small fraction of total faces.
 
-### Per-Pixel Sorting (General Variant)
-Variants that sort all faces projecting onto a pixel at render time, just prior to compositing [S3, unofficial]. They handle visibility cycles gracefully since a visibility sort always exists for each pixel center even if no global sort exists [S3, unofficial]. Methods include scan-line visible surface algorithms with y-buckets, active lists, x-buckets, and z-sorted lists [S3, unofficial]. They usually spend greater effort on per-pixel sorting since exterior faces are a small fraction of total faces [S3, unofficial].
+### XMPVO
+An algorithm that augments MPVO by discovering exterior face relations using ray shooting queries at edge intersections and vertices of the projection complex, then performing a topological sort [S4, unofficial]. It handles non-convex meshes by capturing cross-gap occlusions that MPVO misses [S4, unofficial].
+
+### MPVONC
+A heuristic extension of MPVO to nonconvex meshes that sorts exterior cells with exterior front-facing faces by decreasing distance of centroid to viewpoint, then conducts DFS from each sorted cell [S4, unofficial]. It is used for fast rendering instead of slower correct sorts because correct sorts do not allow real-time interaction for large data sets [S3, book].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** $O(n)$ for convex grids, where $n$ is the number of cells [S1][S3, unofficial]. Each cell is treated twice during initialization and once in the loop; each edge is treated once in initialization and once in the loop. Max 6 faces per cell [S1].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Linear space $O(n)$ for data structures (graph nodes, edges, queues/lists) [S3, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Very fast, accurate for convex meshes, low computational overhead, detects visibility cycles [S3, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Fails for non-convex meshes (requires extra gap relations or approximate methods) [S1][S3, unofficial]. Fails for non-planar faces [S1]. Requires convexity of cells and grid [S1][S3, unofficial].
-*   **When to use:** Convex grids of convex polyhedral cells where exact visibility ordering is required and performance is critical [S1][S3, unofficial].
-*   **When not to use:** Non-convex meshes, non-planar faces, or when real-time interaction with very large non-convex datasets is needed (use Approximate MPVONC or Per-Pixel Sorting) [S2, book][S3, unofficial].
